\DOC LOCAL_MODINV_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MODINV_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-execution goal across a
modular-inversion subroutine call, replacing the current state variable by the
next one and installing the post-call state assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The single {int} argument is the index of the new state: the goal's state
variable is replaced by the state with that number, and the assumptions
describing the pre-call state (indexed by the previous number) are discharged
and replaced by assumptions about the new state. The conclusion keeps its
shape, only the state term it is applied to being advanced to the newly
numbered state. Among the assumptions, those mentioning the old state —
register and memory reads, flag conditions, the program-image assumption and
the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame — are removed, and corresponding assumptions
about the new state are added: a new program counter value, an assumption
giving the memory contents at the output buffer as the modular inverse
(conditionalised on divisibility), re-stated reads of the memory and registers
that survive the call, and an extended {MAYCHANGE} frame covering the registers
and stack region clobbered by the subroutine. Purely mathematical and
nonoverlapping hypotheses are left untouched, and exactly one goal results.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, only this one kind of transformation occurs, always on
goals of this {eventually} machine-execution form with a full set of state
assumptions.

\ENDDOC
